            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>TikTok Shop Cold Launch: The 6 Questions You Must Answer About Creative Pools</h2>
<p>When launching a new product on TikTok Shop, creatives are your ammunition. Insufficient or low-quality assets can cut your scaling speed in half. But many teams face a common problem: they don't know how large their creative pool should be, how to allocate assets, or when to replace underperformers.</p>
<p>Below are 6 real operational questions with direct answers.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Q1: How Many Creatives Do You Need for Cold Launch?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: Start with at least 15-20 effective creatives per series.</strong></p>
<p>These don't all need to be original shoots—you need 15-20 usable versions after editing, shot variation, subtitle localization, and format adaptation. Below this threshold, your testing cycle extends significantly, and the algorithm lacks sufficient data to build stable models.</p>
<p>A case study from a utility app team: they prepared 18 creatives for launch, identified 3 high-CTR creatives within 7 days, and scaled successfully. A team with only 8 creatives saw one single creative consuming 80% of the budget after 14 days—extremely high risk.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Q2: How Should You Allocate Creative Types?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: UGC should account for over 60% during cold launch.</strong></p>
<p>TikTok users respond better to "authentic" content. The algorithm initially favors creatives with higher engagement potential, while brand-focused videos and product说明书-style content typically underperform.</p>
<p>Recommended allocation:<br />
- <strong>UGC Seeding (60%+):</strong> Real-person demos, scenario-based usage, comparison reviews<br />
- <strong>Product Showcase (25%):</strong> Feature close-ups, tutorial demos, benefit-driven voiceovers<br />
- <strong>Brand Proof (15%):</strong> Factory footage, user testimonial compilations, certifications</p>
<p>If your team lacks UGC resources, consider hiring local actors or micro-influencers for baseline shoots. Avoid relying solely on static image carousels.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Q3: What's the Right Cadence for Creative Rotation?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: Don't touch creatives for the first 3 days; start monitoring on Day 4; replace after 7 days of poor performance.</strong></p>
<p>The algorithm needs 3-5 days to accumulate data during cold launch. Premature rotation keeps your account in "learning phase," damaging account weight.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Timeline</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Day 1-3</td>
<td>Monitor, don't change</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Day 4-5</td>
<td>Reduce budget by 10-20% for low-CTR creatives (&lt;1.5%)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Day 7</td>
<td>Replace if CTR stays 50% below average with no conversions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Day 10</td>
<td>Replicate successful creatives, expand pool</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Rule: Don't test more than 5 creatives simultaneously—budget will be too fragmented.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h3>Q4: How to Identify a "Dead Pool" Creative and When to Replace?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: If spend stagnates for 48 hours and CTR drops 20%+ consistently, the creative is dead.</strong></p>
<p>Dead pool signals:<br />
1. Budget is healthy but spend has stalled<br />
2. Impressions suddenly halve<br />
3. CPM doubles unexpectedly</p>
<p>When this happens, first determine the cause: creative fatigue or account suppression. Test a completely new creative. If it starts spending within 3 hours, it's creative fatigue. If the new creative also stalls, it's an account issue requiring AM support or appeal submission.</p>
<p><strong>Once creative fatigue is confirmed, replace immediately—don't hold on.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h3>Q5: How to Differentiate Creatives Across Markets and Categories?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: Localization isn't just subtitle translation—it's re-shooting for local culture.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Vietnam, Thailand):</strong> Prefer fast-paced, scenario-driven content with high saturation colors. Use local trending BGM. Keep videos to 15-30 seconds.</p>
<p><strong>UK/US Markets:</strong> Accept slightly longer narrative content (30-60s), but hook viewers within the first 3 seconds. Use large subtitles. Native speakers or local talent perform better.</p>
<p><strong>Middle East:</strong> Higher female user proportion; home and beauty content performs well. Avoid music with religious implications.</p>
<p><strong>Practical tip: Don't run one creative across all markets. Prepare at least 3 localized versions per primary market—not just subtitle changes.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h3>Q6: What's the Scaling Strategy After a Creative Takes Off?</h3>
<p><strong>Verdict: Expand similar creatives first, then audiences, then placements.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Phase 1 (Within 24 hours of breakout):</strong><br />
- Replicate the winning creative 3-5 times (different opening captions, music, shot sequence)<br />
- Keep the same audience and placement<br />
- Increase budget by 50-100%</p>
<p><strong>Phase 2 (3-5 days after breakout):</strong><br />
- Enable lookalike audience expansion<br />
- Add similar influencer-based audiences</p>
<p><strong>Phase 3 (7 days after breakout):</strong><br />
- Test additional placements (Stories, Search, Marketplace)<br />
- Begin testing new creative types to replenish the pool</p>
<p><strong>Caution: Don't triple your budget overnight. The system will re-enter learning mode, causing cost fluctuations.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>For TikTok Shop cold launches, the creative pool is core infrastructure. Remember three numbers: <strong>15 to start, 60% UGC, 7-day dead pool判断</strong>. Focus on creative quality and rotation cadence, not just quantity.</p>
